    {
      "id": "7637126131446975757",
      "url": "https://www.tiktok.com/@doctorsood/video/7637126131446975757",
      "author_handle": "doctorsood",
      "author_followers": 2700000,
      "caption": "Constipation relief ⬇️\n🌾 Psyllium husk\nPsyllium is a soluble, gel-forming fiber that holds water in the stool, increasing stool softness and bulk. This makes bowel movements easier to pass and can reduce straining. Studies show psyllium is one of the better-supported fiber supplements for chronic constipation, especially when taken consistently with enough water (PMID: 35816465; 30669509).\n💧 Magnesium citrate\nMagnesium citrate acts as an osmotic laxative, pulling water into the intestines and softening stool. This increases bowel movement frequency, but excessive use can cause cramping, diarrhea, dehydration, or electrolyte imbalance, especially in people with kidney disease.\n🦠 Probiotics\nCertain probiotic strains may improve stool frequency, consistency, and transit time. Proposed mechanisms include changes in fermentation, short-chain fatty acid production, and gut motility. Effects vary significantly depending on the strain and the individual (PMID: 25099542; 36570175).\n🥝 Kiwi\nKiwi has surprisingly strong evidence for constipation support. Studies show two green kiwis daily can improve stool frequency and abdominal comfort in people with constipation or IBS-C. Benefits likely come from a combination of fiber, water content, and bioactive compounds (PMID: 36537785; 36247043).\n🍵 Ginger tea\nGinger is better supported for upper GI motility and digestive comfort than constipation itself. It may help gastric emptying and reduce bloating, but the strongest constipation effects are likely coming from fiber and stool-softening strategies (PMID: 18403946; 30680163).\n💡 Takeaway\nRegular bowel movements often improve when stool hydration, fiber bulk, motility, and gut microbiome support work together rather than relying on one single fix.\nFollow @doctorsoood to improve your health IQ and for similar content.\n#GutHealth #ConstipationRelief #DigestiveHealth #Medical #Health",
